WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.406 position:50% size:92% align:center
For the eight-by-eight calculation, a larger

00:00:03.406 --> 00:00:05.383 position:50% size:92% align:center
tile can reduce the algorithm’s

00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:06.723 position:50% size:92% align:center
global operand loads.

00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:09.695 position:50% size:92% align:center
The same change also increases each block’s

00:00:09.695 --> 00:00:12.767 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared storage and thread working state. We need

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:14.687 position:50% size:92% align:center
both sides of that comparison.

00:00:16.707 --> 00:00:19.514 position:50% size:92% align:center
Two shared input tiles need thirty-two bytes per

00:00:19.514 --> 00:00:22.088 position:50% size:92% align:center
block at tile width two, and one hundred and

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:23.960 position:50% size:92% align:center
twenty-eight at tile width four.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.407 position:50% size:92% align:center
These are storage requirements of this

00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:28.166 position:50% size:92% align:center
algorithm, not measurements of

00:00:28.166 --> 00:00:29.748 position:50% size:92% align:center
device-memory transactions.

00:00:31.768 --> 00:00:34.334 position:50% size:92% align:center
Use an imaginary SM with one hundred and

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.093 position:50% size:92% align:center
twenty-eight shared bytes, one thousand and

00:00:37.093 --> 00:00:38.890 position:50% size:92% align:center
twenty-four registers, and a

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.429 position:50% size:92% align:center
maximum of eight blocks.

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:43.517 position:50% size:92% align:center
These intentionally small teaching budgets are

00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:46.073 position:50% size:92% align:center
separate from a real device’s limits. The

00:00:46.073 --> 00:00:47.569 position:50% size:92% align:center
smaller tile allows four

00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:49.439 position:50% size:92% align:center
resident blocks in this model.

00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:54.498 position:50% size:92% align:center
At tile width four, one block consumes the whole

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:57.347 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared-memory budget, so only one block fits.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.493 position:50% size:92% align:center
The reduced operand-load count does not tell us

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.984 position:50% size:92% align:center
whether that loss of resident work helps

00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:04.479 position:50% size:92% align:center
or hurts execution time.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.129 position:50% size:92% align:center
Keep tile width four and the same register

00:01:09.129 --> 00:01:11.696 position:50% size:92% align:center
budget, but raise shared capacity to five

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:13.261 position:50% size:92% align:center
hundred and twelve bytes.

00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:16.189 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether eight blocks must now

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:17.629 position:50% size:92% align:center
fit. Which other resource

00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:19.241 position:50% size:92% align:center
could still limit residency?

00:01:25.061 --> 00:01:27.964 position:50% size:92% align:center
The model permits four blocks, not eight. Each

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:30.424 position:50% size:92% align:center
larger block also needs two hundred and

00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:32.254 position:50% size:92% align:center
fifty-six registers under the

00:01:32.254 --> 00:01:33.957 position:50% size:92% align:center
chosen register assumption.

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:37.585 position:50% size:92% align:center
Use counts to form a hypothesis, verify the full

00:01:37.585 --> 00:01:40.922 position:50% size:92% align:center
numerical result, then measure a named real GPU

00:01:40.922 --> 00:01:42.839 position:50% size:92% align:center
before ranking performance.
